    <!-- Sample conversations shown until the assistant is connected -->
    <string name="sample_parcel_title">Parcel fee text</string>
    <string name="sample_parcel_user">Check this message:\nInPost: Your parcel is on hold. Pay 1.99 PLN within 24h: inpost-dostawa.top/pl</string>
    <string name="sample_parcel_reply">Be careful — this looks like a scam. Don’t tap the link and don’t enter your card details. InPost doesn’t ask for extra fees by text with a link. If you’re expecting a parcel, check its status in the official app. In Poland you can forward suspicious texts to 8080 (CERT Polska).</string>
    <string name="sample_parcel_reason_link">The link leads to a site that doesn’t belong to InPost.</string>
    <string name="sample_parcel_reason_payment">Asking for an extra fee is a common scam trick.</string>
    <string name="sample_parcel_reason_urgency">The message pressures you to act fast.</string>
    <string name="sample_bank_title">Call “from the bank”</string>
    <string name="sample_bank_user">Someone is calling me and says they’re from my bank. What should I do?</string>
    <string name="sample_bank_reply">The safest thing is to hang up now. Your bank will never ask you to move money to a “safe account”, share a code or install an app. If you’re unsure, call your bank yourself using the number on the back of your card.</string>

